.TH CTHREADCOMM 7 "2026-10-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
cthreadcomm \- overview of the thread communication API
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
The module moves data between threads and waits until a queue or a
file descriptor is ready. It has five parts:

  ccol_circular_queue   A bounded queue. A send blocks while the queue
                        is full, which gives backpressure to a producer.
  ccol_dynamic_queue    An unbounded queue. A send never waits.
  ccol_channel          Two circular queues, one in each direction;
                        the identity of the calling thread selects
                        the queue.
  ccol_select()         A one-shot wait for the first ready item among
                        several queues or file descriptors.
  ccol_event_loop       A persistent reactor that runs callbacks for
                        a set of queues and file descriptors that can
                        change.
.fi
.SS Messages and ownership
.nf
Every queue carries c_message_t values:

  typedef struct c_message_t {
      void   *data;
      size_t  size;
  } c_message_t;

The library copies no payload. A send that succeeds gives the
ownership of data to the queue, and the library then sets the data
field of the sender to NULL. A receive gives the ownership to the
receiver, which must free data. A send that fails does not change the
message, so the caller keeps the ownership of data. A message with a
NULL data and a size of 0 is valid; it is the usual end-of-stream
sentinel.
.fi
.SS Blocking, try and timed calls
.nf
A plain send or receive blocks. A try_ call never blocks; instead it
gives ccol_container_full or ccol_container_empty. A timed_ call takes
a uint64_t count of microseconds, which the library measures on
CLOCK_MONOTONIC, so a change to the wall clock does not make the wait
longer or shorter. With a timeout of 0, the timed call operates as its
try_ variant and gives the result of that variant. When a timeout is
too large for a deadline (for example UINT64_MAX), the library uses
the largest deadline, so the deadline never wraps into the past.
.fi
.SS Readiness only
.nf
ccol_select() and ccol_event_loop only tell that an item is ready;
they never receive or send. The caller then immediately makes its own
call that does not block:
  - For a queue: ccol_circq_try_recv_zc(), ccol_dynmq_try_recv_zc(),
    ccol_circq_try_send_zc() or ccol_dynmq_send_zc().
  - For a file descriptor: read(2), recv(2), write(2) or send(2).
As with POSIX select(2), a different consumer or producer can get the
message or the space before that call, so that call must not block,
and you must always examine its result.

An event loop registration can watch a queue. While that registration
has a pending wake, a send to the queue makes no system call, because
one dispatch serves all the sends that came before the dispatch
started.
.fi
.SS Handles
.nf
Queues and channels are plain pointers, and their destroy macros set
the pointer to NULL. ccol_event_loop and ccol_event_reg are opaque
VALUE handles (uint64_t), not pointers; compare them with
CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID and CCOL_EVENT_REG_INVALID, which are both 0.
The library resolves such a handle before it uses the object behind
it, so it refuses a stale handle and never dereferences it.
.fi
.SS Process exit
.nf
The dispatch of each event loop uses two thread-specific keys, which
the library creates one time for each process; queues and channels
need no key. If the process has no free key, the creation of a loop
fails with CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID for the rest of that process, while
queues and channels continue to operate.

The process-exit destructors of the library delete the keys and free
the bookkeeping of the library for all loops and queues. If a queue or
a loop is live at that time, its own destroy does this release later,
so an application destructor that runs after the destructors of the
library can destroy the objects that it owns. After that release, the
creation of a queue, a channel or a loop fails cleanly.
.fi
.SS fork(2)
.nf
The library supports two ways to use fork(2) with this module:
  - Call fork(2) before you create a queue, a channel or a loop.
  - Create the object, then call fork(2) and immediately exec(3).
The library does not support a child that continues with no exec(3)
while an object from before the fork is live. The child never inherits a
lock of a queue in the locked state, and a queue has no thread of its
own, so a queue from the parent stays usable in the child. An event
loop from the parent, however, has no reactor threads in the child, so
it dispatches nothing there.
.fi
